Wave 1 is dual-sourced

Some readers will want to know exactly how the recorded numbers in Table 1 were produced, and for most rows of that table the answer is straightforward: each drafting wave leaves one authoritative trail, and the printed figures come from that single trail. Wave 1 is the exception, and it deserves a fuller explanation, because it is the only wave in the paper whose produced-byte count has two sources that disagree with each other. This section walks through what the two sources recorded, why they diverge, and what the divergence does and does not mean for the rest of the paper.

Wave 1 consisted of two briefs: a paper outline and a related-work draft. The dispatch instrument that ran the wave logged a produced-byte count for each of them, and those two counts sit at the top of the public instrument table, rows 1 and 2. According to that instrument log, the outline brief produced 1,397 bytes and the related-work brief produced 1,438 bytes. Added together, the two give a first-pass total of 2,835 bytes for the wave as a whole. That is the instrument's story: a modest volume of output, logged at the moment of dispatch and carried forward into the paper's table.

The other source is the hub's own audit. At weave time, the hub independently counted what the wave produced. Its audit recorded 22,824 bytes produced and 3,853 characters admitted for wave 1, and from those two counts it derived a hub-audited admitted fraction of 0.17. On the face of it, the two sources are measuring the same thing: how much the wave produced. The instrument says roughly 2.8 thousand bytes across the two briefs; the hub's audit says roughly 22.8 thousand bytes. The disagreement is a factor of about eight.

Why the two are so far apart cannot be settled from the record as it stands, and it is important to be honest about that gap rather than to paper over it. The raw worker outputs for wave 1 were not retained. The audit and the instrument both refer to the same wave, but without the underlying worker text available for direct comparison there is no way to reconcile the two figures, to explain which counting convention each one used, or to determine which of them, if any, reflects what a reader would have actually seen. The factor-of-eight gap is real, documented, and presently unresolved. Anyone trying to reproduce the record should understand that this is not a discrepancy that can be resolved by re-reading a saved file, because no such file was kept. It is a limitation of the surviving record, and it is stated plainly here so that it does not silently mask itself as precision later on.

The paper itself does not hide this. Table 1 prints both figures for wave 1 and marks the row with a note identifying it as dual-sourced. The reader is therefore told, in the table itself, that the numbers in that row come from two sources and are not mutually consistent. That note is the visible acknowledgment that wave 1's byte count is contested, and it is precisely what a careful record should do: present both values, flag that there are two of them, and let the reader weigh them rather than choosing one silently.

There is a further consequence that follows from the instrument's own logging. For the two wave-1 rows, the admitted fraction is undefined on the instrument denominator. The reason is arithmetic rather than mysterious: the brief sizes in the paper, 1,615 bytes for one and 1,741 for the other, exceed the logged transcripts that the instrument recorded for the wave. When the denominator, the logged transcript size, is smaller than the numerator it is being used to normalize, the fraction that would result is not a meaningful ratio. So for these two rows there is no admitted fraction to report on the instrument basis; the cell is left undefined rather than filled with a number that would imply a precision the record does not support.

The important point, and the reason this section can describe the discrepancy without alarming a reader about the integrity of the paper, is that nothing in the paper's cost model or anatomy depends on wave 1 alone. The free-lane corollary needs only the acceptance probability to be above a small threshold; it does not require a precise produced-byte count for any single early wave, and certainly not for the first one. That corollary is robust to the uncertainty here because it rests on a lower-bound style claim that a small threshold is satisfied, not on an exact byte figure that would shift with a different counting convention. Likewise, the per-context-budget bound is stated on the pooled and per-wave admitted fractions, which is a different object from the wave-1 byte totals, and it does not lean on the contested numbers. And the remaining waves, two through four, reproduce from the instrument without any appeal to wave 1 at all; wave 3 reproduces exactly. The weight of the paper's quantitative argument is carried by those later waves and by the pooled figures, all of which trace to a single, self-consistent instrument trail. Wave 1's dual-sourced row is a documented curiosity at the edge of the record, not a load-bearing component of the analysis.

That framing is the honest one to take away. A reader seeking to reproduce the record should treat wave 1's byte counts as what they are: two measurements from two sources at two moments, materially different, and both reported side by side with the row explicitly labeled as dual-sourced. The instrument's first-pass sum of 2,835 bytes across the outline and related-work briefs is the figure a reproduction run would most naturally regenerate, because it is logged and structured. The hub's weave-time audit, at 22,824 bytes produced with an admitted fraction of 0.17, reflects a separate accounting that is not retained in a way that can be independently re-derived. Both are part of the record; neither can be collapsed into the other; and the disagreement, a factor of about eight, is not hidden.

What is missing from the record should be named rather than invented. The raw worker outputs for wave 1 are the piece that would let the two sources be reconciled, and they were not kept. There is consequently no way, from what survives, to say which count better reflects the work actually done, nor to reconstruct it after the fact. This section therefore does not attempt to pick a winner or to reconcile the two figures by assumption. It reports the divergence, notes the factor, explains the undefined admitted fraction on the instrument denominator, and points out that the brief sizes exceed the logged transcripts. And it closes by making clear that this particular row, for all its untidiness, supports none of the paper's load-bearing claims. A reader who reproduces the record and finds two different byte totals for wave 1 has not made a mistake and has not found an error in the paper; they have found the dual-sourcing that Table 1 already announces, and they can proceed with the same confidence into the waves that reproduce cleanly, because the analysis was built to survive exactly this kind of early-record roughness.

